@* FR-30's four statuses, named once.

   Before this component the same four keys were written out on the dispatch board, on the
   own-deliveries screen and would have been again on every timeline entry - three copies of one
   vocabulary, and the kind of duplication where the third copy is the one that goes stale.

   One of StatusLabel's two faces. The chrome - the fill, the shape and the border - belongs to
   DtStatusLabel, which every status vocabulary in the product shares; what belongs here is the
   closed set and its words.

   The face is a total switch rather than a fifth @@if block, and that is the difference from what
   this file used to be: the four arms below decide the word, and a status nobody wrote an arm for
   used to render an empty, unclassed span onto a delivery table and a timeline line. It now fails
   loudly, in the same shape AD-10's own Delivery.NextStatuses fails.

   The words stay independent @@if blocks with their own literal localizer keys: an `else` at markup
   level reads to NFR-14's source scan as a rendered English word, and a key composed at run time
   ("Status" + Status) is a key the closed-catalogue test cannot see. *@
@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer

<DtStatusLabel Face="@Face">
    @if (Status == DeliveryStatus.Pending)
    {
        @Localizer["StatusPending"]
    }
    @if (Status == DeliveryStatus.InTransit)
    {
        @Localizer["StatusInTransit"]
    }
    @if (Status == DeliveryStatus.Delivered)
    {
        @Localizer["StatusDelivered"]
    }
    @if (Status == DeliveryStatus.Failed)
    {
        @Localizer["StatusFailed"]
    }
</DtStatusLabel>

@code {
    /// <summary>The status to label. A closed enum, so a typo is a build error.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public DeliveryStatus Status { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The face the shared label wears for this status.</summary>
    /// <exception cref="ArgumentOutOfRangeException">
    /// A status with no face. Total over the enum like <c>Delivery.NextStatuses</c>: a fifth status
    /// added without a decision about how it is labelled fails here rather than rendering nothing
    /// at all, which is what a table full of blank status cells used to look like.
    /// </exception>
    private DtStatusFace Face => Status switch
    {
        DeliveryStatus.Pending => DtStatusFace.Pending,
        DeliveryStatus.InTransit => DtStatusFace.Transit,
        DeliveryStatus.Delivered => DtStatusFace.Delivered,
        DeliveryStatus.Failed => DtStatusFace.Failed,
        _ => throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(
            nameof(Status),
            Status,
            "No status label is defined for this delivery status."),
    };
}
